    <section aria-label="Article content" class="section-white">
      <div class="site-container section-py">
        <div class="article-prose">
          <p>Dental emergencies can happen at the most unexpected times—and knowing when to seek immediate care can make all the difference in preserving your smile and preventing long-term damage. At <strong>The Center for Smile Enhancement</strong> in <strong>Minnetonka, MN</strong>, we want our patients to feel confident about recognizing what qualifies as a dental emergency and when to take action.</p>

          <h2>What Is Emergency Dentistry?</h2>

          <p><strong>Emergency dentistry</strong> refers to urgent dental care needed to relieve severe pain, stop bleeding, save a tooth, or treat infections that could worsen without timely treatment. While not all dental issues require emergency intervention, some situations demand immediate attention from a qualified dental professional.</p>

          <h2>Common Dental Emergencies We Treat in Minnetonka</h2>

          <p><strong>1. Severe Toothache</strong> If you're experiencing intense, persistent tooth pain that isn't relieved by over-the-counter medication, it could be a sign of an infection, abscess, or deep decay. This is not something to ignore—contact our Minnetonka emergency dentist right away to get relief and avoid more serious complications.</p>

          <p><strong>2. Knocked-Out or Loose Tooth</strong> Accidents or trauma can knock out a tooth or loosen it from the socket. If this happens, try to keep the tooth moist (preferably in milk or saliva) and contact us immediately. Quick action improves the chances of saving the tooth.</p>

          <p><strong>3. Cracked or Broken Teeth</strong> A visibly fractured or chipped tooth, especially if it causes pain or exposes the tooth’s inner layers, is a dental emergency. Cracks can lead to infection and further damage if not addressed promptly.</p>

          <h2>Less Obvious Emergencies You Shouldn’t Ignore</h2>

          <p><strong>1. Oral Cuts and Soft Tissue Injuries</strong> Bleeding cuts or injuries to the gums, lips, tongue, or cheeks should be evaluated quickly. If the bleeding is heavy or doesn’t stop, it’s time to call your emergency dentist in Minnetonka.</p>

          <p><strong>2. Dental Abscess or Swelling</strong> A <strong>dental abscess</strong> is a serious infection that typically presents as a painful, swollen area on the gums, often accompanied by a foul taste, fever, or facial swelling. If left untreated, it can spread quickly and become a serious health concern.</p>

          <p><strong>3. Lost Fillings or Crowns</strong> While it might not seem urgent, a lost crown or filling can expose sensitive parts of your tooth to decay or damage. Contacting us promptly can help prevent further issues and restore your tooth's function.</p>

          <h2>How to Know If It’s a Dental Emergency</h2>

          <p>If you’re unsure whether your dental issue is urgent, ask yourself:</p>

          <ul><li><p><strong>Is there severe or unmanageable pain?</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Has there been recent trauma to the mouth or jaw?</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Is there swelling or a visible infection?</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Are you experiencing excessive bleeding?</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Is a tooth loose, broken, or missing?</strong></p></li></ul>

          <p>If the answer is yes to any of these questions, it’s best to <strong>call The Center for Smile Enhancement immediately</strong>.</p>

          <h2>Final Thoughts from Our Minnetonka Dental Team</h2>

          <p>Understanding what qualifies as a dental emergency can help you act fast and protect your oral health. At <strong>The Center for Smile Enhancement</strong>, we’re here for you when urgent dental needs arise.</p>

          <p><strong>If you're experiencing a dental emergency in Minnetonka, MN</strong>, contact our office right away for immediate care. Prompt treatment can make all the difference in saving your smile and reducing complications.</p>
